+/// Every step of the shuffle draws its own index.
+///
+/// The defect the phase names is specific: the old code computed one `seed` and
+/// used `seed % (i + 1)` for every swap. That still returns a permutation and is
+/// still usually not the identity — so `edge_random_shuffle_permutes_its_input_for_every_seed`
+/// passes on it, and did. What it cannot do is *vary*: the permutation becomes a
+/// fixed function of one draw, so the same seed and length always give the same
+/// answer however many times you ask.
+///
+/// This test asks repeatedly, inside one program, and insists the answers differ.
+/// A Fisher-Yates that draws per step passes it; the one-draw version answers the
+/// same permutation on every iteration and fails.
+///
+/// Note what this is not: it does not claim a particular permutation, or compare
+/// against a golden list. Any fixed check would be a test that encodes the
+/// generator's internals and breaks the next time the mixer changes. The property
+/// is that the shuffle *consumes* the stream — one draw cannot produce two
+/// different answers.
+#[test]
+fn edge_random_shuffle_draws_a_fresh_index_at_every_step() {
+    let source = format!(
+        "random_seed({SEED})\n\
+         set seen to []\n\
+         for each i from 1 to 40\n    \
+             append(\"seen\", random_shuffle([1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8]))\n\
+         end\n\
+         seen"
+    );
+    let Value::List(items) = eval(&source) else {
+        panic!("the shuffle loop should answer a list");
+    };
+    assert_eq!(
+        items.len(),
+        40,
+        "the loop should have produced 40 shuffles, it produced {}",
+        items.len()
+    );
